#include (../Settings/defs.ves)

In Volume 1, we explored basic terminologies and their properties of metric
spaces. In this chapter, we will extend those by introducing the concept called
\lq\lq topology\rq\rq.

useenv defin {
    Let $X$ be a set. A \it{topology}\index{topology} is a collection $\tau$ of
    some subsets of $X$ satisfies the following conditions.
    useenv enumerate [label=(\roman*)] {
        \item $\emptyset,X\in\tau$;
        \item If $\Us\subset\tau$, then $\bigcup\Us\in\tau$;
        \item If $\Fs\subset\tau$ be a finite subcollection, then
        $\bigcap\Fs\in\tau$.
    }

    Every element of a topology is called an \it{open set}\index{set!open}, and
    complement of open sets are called \it{closed sets}\index{set!closed}.
}

Everywhere in this book, \bf{topological spaces are not empty}.
Below exercise gives a tool whether a set is an open set. We will use the
following exercise without mentioning afterwords.

#label(exe:open-sets)
useenv exec* {
    Let $X$ be a topological space and $E$ be a nonempty subset of $X$ such that
    for every $x\in E$, there exists an open set $U$ such that $x \in U \subset
    E$. Prove that $E$ is open in $X$.
}

By the definition of closed sets, we can clearly obtain the following.

useenv prop {
    useenv enumerate [label=(\roman*)] {
        \item If $\Us$ is a collection of some closed sets, then $\bigcap\Us$
        is also closed.
        \item If $\Fs$ is a finite collection of some closed sets, then
        $\bigcup\Fs$ is closed.
    }
}

If $X$ is a nonempty set, one can define several topologies on $X$.
Let us consider a collection $\Tc$ of some topologies of $X$.
Then interestingly, the intersection $\bigcap\Tc$ is also a topology of $X$.
Here is the proposition for it.

#label(prop:intersection-topologies)
useenv prop {
    Let $X$ be a nonempty set and $\Tc$ a collection of some topologies of $X$.
    Then $\bigcap\Tc$ is also a topology of $X$.
    \proof
    Since each element of $\Tc$ is a topology of $X$, clearly $\emptyset$ and $X$
    belongs in $\bigcap\Tc$.
    If $\Uc\subset\bigcap\Tc$, then $\Uc\subset\tau$ for every $\tau\in\Tc$.
    Since $\tau$ is a topology, $\bigcup\Uc\in\tau$ for all $\tau\in\Tc$,
    hence $\bigcup\Uc\in\bigcap\Tc$.
    Similarly, if $\Fc$ is a finite subcollection of $\bigcap\Tc$,
    one can prove that $\bigcap\Fc\in\bigcap\Tc$. Therefore, $\bigcap\Tc$ is a topology. \proved
}

Consider a collection $\Ec$ of some subsets of $X$ and $\Tc$ a collection of all topologies
which contains $\Ec$. Since $\Pc(X)$ is clearly a topology and $\Ec\subset\Pc(X)$, the collection
$\Tc$ is not empty.
Thanks to \Cref{prop:intersection-topologies}, $\bigcap\Tc$ is a topology of $X$, and
$\bigcap\Tc \supset \Ec$. By the definition of $\Tc$, $\bigcap\Tc$ is contained in every
topology which contains $\Ec$. For this perspective, we can say that $\bigcap\Tc$
is the smallest topology which contains $\Ec$. Thus the following definition makes sense.

#label(defin:subbasis)
useenv defin [Subbasis] {
    Let $X$ be a nonempty set and $\Ec$ a collection of some subsets of $X$.
    Then the smallest topology which contains $\Ec$ exists. We say that
    topology as a one \it{generated by $\Ec$}, and is denoted by $\tau(\Ec)$.
    
    Furthermore, if $(X,\tau)$ is a topology and there exists a collection $\Ec$
    of subsets of $X$ such that $\tau(\Ec) = \tau$, then we say that $\Ec$ a
    \it{subbasis}\index{subbasis} of $\tau$ (or $X$).
}
useenv remark {
    Let $X$ be a topological space and $\Ec$ be a subbasis of the topology on $X$.
    Then the definition of a subbasis gives that every element of $\Ec$ is open.
}

Using \Cref{defin:subbasis}, one can easily define a topology with desired open
sets equipped. However, there exists some special subbasis which gives not only
generates a topology, but also gives an analogous of \Cref{exe:open-sets}.

#label(defin:basis)
useenv defin [Basis and Local Basis] {
    Let $(X,\tau)$ be a topological space, and consider a collection $\Bc$ of
    some subsets of $X$ such that
    #enum ((\alph*)) {
        \item For each $x\in X$, there exists $V\in\Bc$ such that $V\ni x$;
        \item If $U,V\in\Bc$ and $x\in U\cap V$, there exists $W\in\Bc$
        such that $x\in W$ and $W\subset U\cap V$.
    }
    Then $\Bc$ is called a \it{basis}\index{basis} for $\tau$ if $\tau =
    \tau(\Bc)$.

    A \it{local basis}\index{basis!local} for $\tau$ at $x\in X$ is a collection
    $\Bc\subset\tau$ such that
    #enum ((\alph*)) {
        \addtocounter{#enum_counter}{2}
        \item $x\in V$ for all $V\in\Bc$;
        \item If $V$ is an open set containing $x$, then there exists $W\in\Bc$
        such that $W\subset V$.
    }
}
useenv remark {
    Let $X$ be a topological space and $\Bc$ be a basis of the topology on $X$.
    Then the definition of a basis gives that every element of $\Bc$ is open.
}

Here is an analogous of \Cref{exe:open-sets} if a topological space has a basis.

#label(prop:open-sets-basis)
useenv prop {
    Let $X$ be a topological space and $\Bc$ be a basis of the topology on $X$.
    Then $U\subset X$ is open if and only if for every $x\in U$, there exists
    $V\in\Bc$ such that $x\in V\subset U$.
    \proof
    Consider
    #eq (eq:prop:open-sets-basis) {
        \tau = \{U\subset X : \forall x\in U,\exists V\in\Bc
        #"such that"# x\in V\subset U\}.
    }
    By \Cref{exe:prop:open-sets-basis}, $\tau$ is a topology for $X$.
    The definition of $\tau$ clearly implies $\Bc\subset\tau$, hence we obtain
    $\tau(\Bc)\subset\tau$.
    This implies that every open set $U$ is contained in $\tau$, thus we proved
    the one side.

    Conversely, fix any $U\in\tau$. Further assume that $U$ is not empty,
    otherwise it is clear that $U$ is open.
    For each $x\in U$ associates $V_x\in\Bc$ such that $x\in V_x \subset
    U$. Thus we have
    $$
        U = \bigcup_{x\in U} V_x.
    $$
    Since each $V_x$ is open, the right hand side is also open, thus $U$ is also
    open. Therefore, $U\in\tau(\Bc)$, and thus $\tau\subset\tau(\Bc)$.
    This proves the proposition. \proved
}

#label(exe:prop:open-sets-basis)
useenv exec {
    Prove that $\tau$ defined at \cref{eq:prop:open-sets-basis} is a topology
    for $X$.
}

If $X$ is a topological space, we should define closures and interiors
differently. However, if one studied well about closures and interiors in metric
spaces, below definition makes sense.

useenv defin [Closures and Interiors] {
  Let $X$ be a topological space and $A$ a subset of $X$. Then a
  \it{closure}\index{closure} of $A$ is an intersection of all closed sets each
  of which contains $A$, and an \it{interior}\index{interior} of $A$ is a union
  of all open sets each of which is contained in $A$.

  We write a closure of $A$ by $\cl{A}$\index[sym]{$\cl{A}$}, and an interior of
  $A$ by $A^\circ$\index[sym]{$A^\circ$}.
}

Below exercise can be proved directly from the definition of closures and
interiors. Thus in the entire of this book, we use the below exercise without
mention it.

useenv exec* {
    Prove the followings.
    #enum ((\alph*)) {
        \item For every set $A$, $\overline{A}$ is closed, and $A^\circ$ is
        open.
        \item $E = \overline{E}$ if and only if $E$ is closed, and $U = U^\circ$ if
        and only if $U$ is open.
    }
}

There is a relationship between closures and interiors.
#label(prop:closure-and-interior)
useenv prop {
    Let $X$ be a topological space and $A$ a subset of $X$.
    Then $\overline{X-!A} = X -! A^\circ$, and $(X-!A)^\circ = X -! \overline{A}$.
    \proof
    The second one follows from the first one. Indeed,
    $$
        \overline{A} = \overline{X -! (X -! A)} = X -! (X -! A)^\circ.
    $$
    By taking their complement, we obtain the second one from the first one.
    
    To prove the first one, fix any closed set $F \supset X -! A$.
    Then we have $X -! F \subset A$. Since $X -! F$ is open, by the definition of an interior,
    $X -! F \subset A^\circ$, which implies $X -! A^\circ \subset F$.
    Since this holds for every closed set which contains $X -! A$, the definition of
    a closure implies $X -! A^\circ \subset \overline{X-! A}$.
    
    On the other hand, clearly $A^\circ \subset A$ and $A^\circ$ is open.
    Hence, $X -! A \subset X -! A^\circ$, and $X -! A^\circ$ is closed.
    Therefore, by the definition of a closure, $\overline{X-!A} \subset X -!
    A^\circ$ is obtained. The proof is now finished. \proved
}

#label(exe:arith-closure)
useenv exec* {
    Prove that $\overline{A\cap B} \subset \overline{A} \cap \overline{B}$ and
    $\overline{A\cup B} = \overline{A} \cup \overline{B}$.
    How about infinite union and intersection, respectively?
}

Now we define neighborhoods of a point in topological spaces. Although we defined
this to be open in metric spaces, for some purpose, we define neighborhoods \it{need
not to be open}.

#label(defin:nbhd)
useenv defin [Neighborhoods] {
  Let $X$ be a topological space and $x \in X$. Then a subset $N \subset X$ is
  called a \it{neighborhood of $x$}\index{neighborhood} if its interior contains
  $x$. That is, $x \in N^\circ$. For each $x\in X$, define
  $\Nc_x$\index[sym]{$\Nc_x$} a collection of all \it{open} neighborhoods of
  $x$.
}

Now we are going to study a closure more deeply. Using the definition of a closure,
it is hard to show that whether a point lie in the closure or not.
Below proposition explains the way to check such things.

#label(prop:closure-nbhd)
useenv prop {
    Let $X$ be a topological space, and $A$ a nonempty subset of $X$.
    Then $x\in \overline{A}$ if and only if for every open neighborhood $V$ of $x$,
    $V$ and $A$ intersects.
    \proof
    Fix $x\in\overline{A}$ and an open neighborhood $V$ of $x$.
    If $V\cap A = \emptyset$, then $A\subset X -! V$. Since $X -! V$ is closed,
    by the definition of the closure, we must have $\overline{A}\subset X-!V$.
    However, in this case, we obtain
    $$
        x \in \overline{A} \cap V = \emptyset,
    $$
    which is a contradiction. Thus $V$ and $A$ should intersect.
    
    Conversely, if $x\notin\overline{A}$, then $x\in X -! \overline{A} = (X -!
    A)^\circ$ because of \Cref{prop:closure-and-interior}.
    Observe that $(X-!A)^\circ$ is an open neighborhood of $x$ which is
    disjoint with $\overline{A}$, hence with $A$.
    Therefore, we found an open neighborhood of $x$ which is disjoint with $A$
    provided that $x\notin\overline{A}$. \proved
}
